======================================================
4. A SKILL IS JUST A FOLDER
======================================================
SAY: "A skill is just a folder with a Markdown file
      in it."  (pause - this surprises people)
- Point at the description line: "Most important line.
  The agent reads it to decide whether to use the
  skill: what it does and when to use it."
- "Below that, the procedure in plain English.
  Optional scripts and docs."
- "This one is real: read-only Kubernetes triage. It
  never touches the cluster; it tells you the next
  command to run."
> "It's just files, so it lives in git and gets
   reviewed like code. What happens when an agent has
   a lot of them?"

======================================================
5. PROGRESSIVE DISCLOSURE
======================================================
SAY: "An agent only reads what the task needs."
- "Like a table of contents. At startup it reads only
  the titles, about 100 tokens per skill. When a task
  matches, it opens that one chapter. Scripts and docs
  only if a step needs them."
- "So we can give it hundreds of skills without
  slowing it down or running up the bill."
> "And these skills aren't tied to one product."

